    {
      "external_id": "C11-CHEM-CH8-8.8",
      "chapter": "pk-punjab/xi/chemistry/c11-chem/ch8",
      "topic": "pk-punjab/xi/chemistry/c11-chem/ch8/8.8",
      "title": "8.8 Equilibrium constant and position of equilibrium",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "b07b1f6c-f5a4-509d-ae86-73be5bffb0d4",
          "pdf_from": 173,
          "pdf_to": 174,
          "note": "Topic 8.8, printed pages 173-174."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The position of equilibrium refers to the relative amounts of products and reactants present in an equilibrium mixture."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "In 1864, the Norwegian chemists C. Guldberg (1836–1902) and P. Waage (1833–1900) carefully measured the compositions of many reaction systems at equilibrium. They discovered that for any reversible reaction, the ratio of the product of the equilibrium concentrations of the products (raised to their coefficients in the balanced chemical equation) to the product of the equilibrium concentrations of the reactants (raised to their coefficients in the balanced chemical equation) is always a constant under a given set of conditions."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Consider the general reaction:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "a\\mathrm{A} + b\\mathrm{B} \\rightleftharpoons c\\mathrm{C} + d\\mathrm{D}",
            "text_alt": "aA + bB ⇌ cC + dD"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "where A and B are reactants, C and D are products, and a, b, c, and d are the stoichiometric coefficients in the balanced chemical equation."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "K_c = \\frac{[\\mathrm{C}]^c\\,[\\mathrm{D}]^d}{[\\mathrm{A}]^a\\,[\\mathrm{B}]^b}",
            "text_alt": "Kc = [C]ᶜ[D]ᵈ / [A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The rate of the chemical reaction is directly proportional to the active masses of the reactants, raised to the coefficients present in the balanced equation. By the term active mass, we mean the molar concentration. It is expressed in moles dm⁻³. This concentration is expressed by square brackets [ ]."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Let us consider a general reaction"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\mathrm{A} + \\mathrm{B} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{C} + \\mathrm{D}",
            "text_alt": "A + B ⇌ C + D"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "According to the Law of mass action,"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Rate of forward reaction} \\propto [\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}]",
            "text_alt": "Rate of forward reaction ∝ [A][B]"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "= k_f\\,[\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}]",
            "text_alt": "= kf [A][B]"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "‘kf’ is the proportionality constant, and is known as forward rate constant."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Rate of reverse reaction} \\propto [\\mathrm{C}][\\mathrm{D}]",
            "text_alt": "Rate of reverse reaction ∝ [C][D]"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "= k_r\\,[\\mathrm{C}][\\mathrm{D}]",
            "text_alt": "= kr [C][D]"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "‘kr’ is the proportionality constant and is known as reverse rate constant."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "At the equilibrium stage, the forward and the reverse rates are equal. Hence,"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "k_f[\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}] = k_r[\\mathrm{C}][\\mathrm{D}]",
            "text_alt": "kf[A][B] = kr[C][D]"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\frac{k_f}{k_r} = \\frac{[\\mathrm{C}][\\mathrm{D}]}{[\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}]}",
            "text_alt": "kf / kr = [C][D] / [A][B]"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The left side of this equation is the ratio of two rate constants, so it gives another constant called the equilibrium constant (Kc)."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "K_c = \\frac{k_f}{k_r}",
            "text_alt": "Kc = kf / kr"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{So,}\\quad K_c = \\frac{[\\mathrm{C}][\\mathrm{D}]}{[\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}]}",
            "text_alt": "So, Kc = [C][D] / [A][B]"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "This equation is known as equilibrium constant expression."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Consider the following reversible reaction,"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "a\\mathrm{A} + b\\mathrm{B} \\rightleftharpoons c\\mathrm{C} + d\\mathrm{D}",
            "text_alt": "aA + bB ⇌ cC + dD"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "where a, b, c and d are the coefficients of balanced equation."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Then}\\quad K_c = \\frac{[\\mathrm{C}]^c\\,[\\mathrm{D}]^d}{[\\mathrm{A}]^a\\,[\\mathrm{B}]^b}",
            "text_alt": "Then Kc = [C]ᶜ[D]ᵈ / [A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ"
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
